// wire.bs -- refinements and the interval patterns that had to land with them
// (F2, tickets 20 §5, 42, 43 and 12 §2).
//
//     bsc examples/Wire/wire.bs Classify 6     -> :reserved
//     bsc examples/Wire/wire.bs Classify 8     -> :heartbeat
//     bsc examples/Wire/wire.bs Band 200       -> :high
//
// THE PROPERTY WORTH LOOKING AT is that `Classify` below is exhaustive over 256
// values with no catch-all -- and that it could not have been written at all a
// feature ago. `type Octet` is the first CLOSED numeric domain this language can
// spell, and the moment a domain closes, ticket 12 §2 makes `_` an error over it.
// So the refinement and the span pattern are one change: shipping the first
// without the second would have turned working programs into rejected ones.
//
// Delete `Classify(>= 4 and <= 7)` and the compiler hands the same line straight
// back as the clause to add -- the residual IS the missing case, spelled since
// F29 the way you would write it. `bin/check-residual-pasteable.sh` runs that
// sentence: it deletes every `Classify` clause below in turn and reads the reply.
module Wire

// A REFINEMENT, ticket 20 §5. `value` names the thing being refined; it is an
// ordinary name rather than a keyword, so a parameter may still be called
// `value` and the word means the subject only inside a `where`.
//
// This is a SUBSET of `int`, not a type beside it, so nothing new entered the
// algebra -- and the emitted `-spec` says `0..255` where it used to say
// `integer()` whatever the clauses tested.
type Octet = int where value >= 0 and value <= 255

type FrameType = :method | :header | :body | :heartbeat | :reserved

// A RELATIONAL PATTERN, ticket 42. `4..7` was refused: C#'s `..` builds a
// half-open slice over *indices*, is not enumerable, and in pattern position
// already means "the rest" -- which this language took for lists in ticket 28
// and runs in `fib.bs` as `Reverse([x, ..rest], acc)`. The rule that came out of
// it: borrow the construct, or don't borrow the glyph.
//
// `and` is the same conjunction a guard uses (ticket 44). One spelling, in every
// position -- which matters here more than in C#, because this language puts
// patterns in the parameter position and so a pattern and a guard sit one line
// apart rather than in different constructs.
public FrameType Classify(Octet)

Classify(1)             -> :method
Classify(2)             -> :header
Classify(3)             -> :body
Classify(8)             -> :heartbeat
Classify(0)             -> :reserved
Classify(>= 4 and <= 7) -> :reserved
Classify(>= 9)          -> :reserved

// THE TWO REFINEMENTS AGREE, which is the thing that would be easy to get wrong
// and silent if it were. `n` is declared `Octet` and the clause also guards on
// it, so the residual after the first clause is `0..128` and not `int <= 128` --
// the type's bound and the guard's bound are read by the SAME translator, so
// they cannot come to disagree.
//
// Three clauses, no catch-all, over a closed domain.
type Size = :low | :mid | :high

public Size Band(Octet n)

Band(n) when n > 128 -> :high
Band(n) when n > 64  -> :mid
Band(n) when n <= 64 -> :low

// A span in a SWITCH ARM, because an arm is the clause head's own pattern
// grammar one level down (F7, ticket 17 §6). Nothing here is a second
// implementation of anything above it.
public Size Sizing(Octet n)

Sizing(n) -> n switch {
    >= 129           => :high,
    >= 65 and <= 128 => :mid,
    <= 64            => :low
}
